# Target package.use — per-package flags for the image build (plan/03).

sys-apps/systemd            boot policykit
# sudo[sendmail] (default on) pulls virtual/mta so sudo can mail failed-attempt notices; the
# first provider is mail-mta/nullmailer, which drags a mail spool, an nullmail user and an
# sendmail symlink into a desktop image that has no business relaying mail. It also breaks the
# build outright: its pkg_postinst runs "chown nullmail:nullmail" against $ROOT, but chown
# resolves users from the BUILDER's passwd where that user does not exist
# ("chown: invalid user: nullmail"). Off — no MTA in the image (plan/03: nothing like this in
# the native set).
app-admin/sudo              -sendmail
# kernel-open was removed upstream (the open GSP modules are unconditional now), but
# dist-kernel is still a real flag — it comes from linux-mod-r1.eclass, not this ebuild's own
# IUSE, and it is REQUIRED here: without it the ebuild builds modules against a configured
# /usr/src/linux, which an image built from gentoo-kernel-bin does not have ("Unable to find
# kernel sources at $TARGET/usr/src/linux"). With it, modules are built against
# virtual/dist-kernel, which is plan/03's "prebuilt against gentoo-kernel-bin".
# -X: plan/03 is Wayland-only, but nvidia-drivers[X] installs the X DDX (nvidia_drv.so,
# libglx.so) and with it pulls x11-base/xorg-server -> x11-base/xorg-drivers -> every
# xf86-video-* matching VIDEO_CARDS (amdgpu, ati, nouveau) plus xf86-input-libinput and the
# x11-apps client utilities (xinit, xauth, xhost, xset, xrdb, iceauth). A full X server, in an
# image whose session is Wayland-only and whose GDM is configured WaylandEnable=true with no
# Xorg fallback. NVIDIA's Wayland path is GBM/EGLStream driven by nvidia-drm.modeset=1, which
# is already on the UKI cmdline in stage 40, and x11-base/xwayland stays in @desktop, so X11
# Flatpak apps keep working. Revert this line if GDM ever needs a real Xorg fallback on NVIDIA
# (plan/08 open question 4) — it is the only thing standing between here and that server.
# -static-libs: .a archives have no consumer in an image with no linker.
x11-drivers/nvidia-drivers  dist-kernel -X -static-libs
sys-kernel/linux-firmware   redistributable -initramfs
media-video/pipewire        sound-server pipewire-alsa
# modemmanager is required by gnome-control-center[networkmanager] (>=1.52.0[modemmanager]
# plus net-misc/modemmanager itself) — without it the Settings "Network" panel cannot be
# built at all. Enabled deliberately: one extra daemon is cheaper than losing the panel.
net-misc/networkmanager     wifi modemmanager -bluetooth
media-libs/mesa             vulkan vaapi wayland
media-libs/libcanberra      -udev
# installkernel stays inert in the target: no initramfs/bootloader hooks at emerge time —
# the UKI is built from the builder (stage 40), not by package hooks.
sys-kernel/installkernel    -dracut -grub -systemd-boot
# ...and the kernel must not try to build one either. gentoo-kernel-bin[initramfs] (on by
# default) runs a generation step in pkg_postinst that refuses to work with ROOT set:
# "ROOT is set, and (re-)generation of an initramfs is requested via the USE=initramfs flag.
# However, this is currently not supported via the sys-kernel/installkernel mechanism."
# Stage 40 builds the real initrd with dracut --sysroot and folds it into the UKI, so the
# ebuild's copy would be discarded anyway. (-generic-uki for the same reason: our UKI is
# assembled by ukify in stage 40, with our cmdline and os-release.)
sys-kernel/gentoo-kernel-bin -initramfs -generic-uki

# ---- GNOME session ----------------------------------------------------------------
# plan/03 wants the Flatpak backend only. NB: the firmware flag is named "firmware" (not
# "fwupd") and malcontent is no longer a USE flag at all (hardcoded off in the ebuild), so
# the old "-fwupd -malcontent" masked nothing and sys-apps/fwupd — explicitly deferred to
# the roadmap in plan/03 — was being pulled into the image.
gnome-extra/gnome-software        flatpak -firmware -dkms -snap
# -X is what actually removes the Xorg server. Traced from the target VDB: gdm[X] RDEPENDs
# x11-base/xorg-server, which PDEPENDs x11-base/xorg-drivers, which pulls every xf86-video-*
# matching VIDEO_CARDS (amdgpu, ati, nouveau) plus xf86-input-libinput — and those in turn
# RDEPEND xorg-server right back. That is a complete X server inside a session plan/03 defines
# as Wayland-only, with GDM already configured WaylandEnable=true and no Xorg session reachable.
# nvidia-drivers[-X] above removes nvidia from the same reverse-dep set but is NOT sufficient
# on its own; gdm is the other half. x11-base/xwayland stays in @desktop and mutter[X] keeps
# X11 client support, so X11 Flatpak apps are unaffected — what goes is the standalone server.
gnome-base/gdm                    wayland -X -xdmcp -fprint -plymouth
# -X is the THIRD and last flag holding the Xorg server in. Traced from the 49.9 ebuild:
#   X? ( >=x11-drivers/xf86-input-libinput-0.19.0 )
# and that driver RDEPENDs x11-base/xorg-server, which PDEPENDs x11-base/xorg-drivers, which
# pulls xf86-video-{amdgpu,ati,nouveau} — each of which RDEPENDs xorg-server right back. The
# stack is a cycle, so it survives until every entry point is closed: nvidia-drivers[-X] and
# gdm[-X] above are the other two, and removing either alone changed nothing.
# The panels keep working: dev-libs/libinput is an UNCONDITIONAL dep of this package (the X
# flag only adds the X11 driver on top), and gtk:4[X,wayland] is required either way, so
# Xwayland clients are unaffected.
gnome-base/gnome-control-center   -X -gnome-online-accounts
# evolution-data-server is a hard RDEPEND of gnome-shell (calendar). These flags are what
# keeps net-libs/webkit-gtk — a multi-hour, ~300 MB build — out of the native image.
# NB: the flag is oauth-gtk3/oauth-gtk4 as of EDS 3.60 — plain "oauth" is no longer in IUSE,
# so the old "-oauth" silently stopped masking anything and webkit-gtk:4.1 came back in via
# the gtk3 OAuth UI. Both variants are disabled: gtk3 pulls webkit-gtk:4.1, gtk4 pulls :6.
gnome-extra/evolution-data-server -oauth-gtk3 -oauth-gtk4 -kerberos -ldap -weather -vala

# nautilus[previewer] pulls gnome-extra/sushi → app-text/evince → app-text/poppler (and a
# second gtk+:3 stack). plan/03 keeps document viewers out of the native image — they are
# Flatpak territory — so the previewer goes with them.
gnome-base/nautilus               -previewer

# nautilus search is backed by tinysparql[localsearch]; localsearch[gstreamer] drags
# gst-plugins-meta (mpeg2dec, dvd, vcd) and media-libs/libsdl2 in for media metadata
# extraction. Indexing and search stay; the codec stack is Flatpak territory (plan/03).
app-misc/localsearch              -gstreamer
x11-wm/mutter                     wayland X screencast

# GNOME/GTK pull in binary packages from the official binhost that were built with a more
# minimal (non-desktop) USE profile than ours; these force the matching source builds
# instead of a mismatched binary, per portage's own conflict-resolution suggestions.
x11-libs/libdrm             video_cards_radeon
dev-libs/glib               dbus introspection
# vulkan: gtkmm-4.20.0-r1 depends on >=gui-libs/gtk-4.19.4:4[vulkan?] and is itself built with
# vulkan (global USE), so gtk must carry the flag explicitly — otherwise the two roots resolve
# gtk differently and portage backtracks until "All ebuilds that could satisfy
# >=gui-libs/gtk-4.19.4:4[vulkan?] have been masked". This is portage's own suggested fix.
gui-libs/gtk                wayland X introspection vulkan
# gtk[vulkan,X,wayland] requires >=media-libs/vulkan-loader-1.3:=[wayland?,X?], so the loader
# has to carry the same two flags or the graph cannot resolve.
media-libs/vulkan-loader    X wayland
x11-libs/gtk+               wayland X introspection
x11-libs/cairo              X
sys-auth/polkit             gtk introspection
x11-libs/libxkbcommon       X
media-video/ffmpeg          vaapi
net-print/cups               dbus
x11-base/xwayland           libei
app-crypt/gcr               gtk
media-libs/libpulse         X
media-plugins/alsa-plugins  pulseaudio
media-libs/freetype         harfbuzz
media-libs/libglvnd         X
sys-libs/minizip-ng         compat

# gnupg stays in @base for the sysupdate verify path (plan/03), but app-alternatives/gpg
# pulls app-crypt/pinentry, whose qt6 flag drags dev-qt/qtbase + kde-frameworks/kwindowsystem
# into a GNOME-only image. gcr already provides the GNOME pinentry; keep the GTK one as the
# console/graphical fallback and leave Qt out entirely.
app-crypt/pinentry          gtk ncurses -qt6 -efl -emacs

# xdg-utils drags dev-perl/Net-DBus + dev-perl/X11-Protocol, and Net-DBus brings XML-Twig and
# the whole LWP/HTML perl stack behind it — ~37 packages. What it buys is the perl
# xdg-screensaver implementation talking X11, which is useless in a Wayland-only session where
# GNOME apps inhibit idle through portals. xdg-utils itself stays: it is a hard RDEPEND of
# gnome-shell and gdm.
#
# -gnome is load-bearing and "-perl" is not the flag that controls this. In 1.2.1-r11 the guard
# reads
#     dbus? ( sys-apps/dbus  gnome? ( dev-perl/Net-DBus  dev-perl/X11-Protocol ) )
#     perl? ( dev-perl/File-MimeInfo )
# so "perl" gates only File-MimeInfo, while the ~37-package LWP/XML stack hangs off gnome —
# and dbus, which this image needs. Keeping -perl is still right (File-MimeInfo is dead weight
# here), but on its own it does not hold that stack out.
#
# Scope of the claim, deliberately narrow: the previous image did NOT ship these packages
# (expected-packages.txt has dev-perl/Parse-Yapp and nothing else), so this is not a
# long-standing leak. It surfaced on a from-scratch rebuild, where xdg-utils resolved to a
# binhost binpkg built with gnome+dbus and its baked RDEPEND pulled Net-DBus in. -gnome makes
# the exclusion deterministic instead of dependent on which binpkg the binhost happens to
# serve. What it costs is xdg-utils' GNOME-specific detection path in xdg-open/xdg-screensaver;
# xdg-open still resolves handlers through gio, and idle inhibition here goes through portals.
x11-misc/xdg-utils          -perl -gnome

# cairomm defaults to -X and depends on cairo[X=], which drags a second, X-less cairo binary
# into a graph where mutter/gtk/pango all require cairo[X]. Match it to our cairo[X].
dev-cpp/cairomm             X

# localsearch[pdf] (nautilus file indexing) needs poppler's cairo backend for PDF thumbnails
# and metadata. Keeping indexing means keeping this on; poppler is a library, not a viewer.
app-text/poppler            cairo

# poppler defaults to qt6, which drags dev-qt/qtbase into a GNOME-only image for a library
# only ever used headlessly here (PDF indexing).
app-text/poppler            -qt6

# ---- printer panel cascade -----------------------------------------------------------
# gnome-control-center[cups] pulls app-admin/system-config-printer (the org.fedoraproject
# .Config.Printing D-Bus service the panel calls), which brings net-fs/samba for SMB printer
# discovery and dev-python/pillow via docutils. Kept deliberately so plan/03's "control-center
# absorbs printer setup" holds; these flags are what the resulting graph needs to resolve.
media-libs/tiff             jpeg
net-fs/samba                client
net-libs/ngtcp2             gnutls

# libb2[openmp] RDEPENDs on "|| ( sys-devel/gcc[openmp] llvm-runtimes/clang-runtime[openmp] )"
# for the OpenMP runtime, which portage satisfies by putting a full sys-devel/gcc in the
# image — breaking plan/06's toolchain-free guarantee. The flag is profile-inherited, not
# ours, and it only parallelizes BLAKE2bp hashing. Off.
# The last two Qt entry points, and the reason dev-qt/{qtbase,qtsvg,qttranslations} were in the
# image despite -qt6 on poppler and pinentry above. appstream[qt6] builds AppStreamQt (the
# binding KDE Discover uses) and libportal[qt6] builds libportal-qt6 for Qt portal clients;
# nothing in a GNOME-only image links either one. gnome-software and xdg-desktop-portal-gnome
# consume the GLib bindings from these same two packages, which are unaffected. qtbase was
# arriving fully loaded — USE="X cups gtk gui opengl sql sqlite vulkan wayland widgets ..." —
# for zero consumers. libportal[-vala] for the same reason stage 50 deletes usr/share/vala:
# .vapi files are build-time inputs.
dev-libs/appstream          -qt6
dev-libs/libportal          -qt6 -vala
# ...and the package that actually kept dev-qt/qtbase in the image. The flag is "gui", not
# "qt6": wpa_supplicant[gui] builds wpa_gui and RDEPENDs dev-qt/qtbase:6[gui,widgets] plus
# dev-qt/qtsvg unconditionally, so disabling qt6 everywhere else changed nothing. (Same shape
# as the "firmware" not "fwupd" and "oauth-gtk3" not "oauth" corrections above — a -qt6 that
# silently masks nothing.) wpa_gui is a standalone Qt supplicant UI; NetworkManager owns the
# network here and GNOME Settings is the UI, so nothing would ever launch it.
net-wireless/wpa_supplicant -gui

app-crypt/libb2             -openmp
